# shir_nodes/address_of.node — AddressOf: reference-taking (`&x`).
#
# GENERIC (Go &x, Zig &x, C-like frontends). The IR passes VALUES; what
# "taking an address" means is backend-defined:
#   - backends with real references render a genuine reference
#     (Perl: \(...) / \(...) — aliasing preserved);
#   - value-only backends (JS/ESTree) use SNAPSHOT semantics: the
#     current value passes through. Writes through such a reference are
#     NOT representable there — a frontend must refuse them at its own
#     gate (Refuse > guess), not mis-lower.
# Deref is the matching read side; keep the pair's contract in sync.

node AddressOf
tag "AddressOf"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field operand: expr
# Spec: TextOps#address_of_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "CountedFor, Spread, AddressOf/Deref, CloneDeep: value-level laws"). An address denotes
# a CELL of a minimal heap (address → bytes). Proved: a write through an address followed
# by a read of that address returns exactly the stored bytes, and writing back what was
# read leaves the heap untouched — the roundtrips a backend that rendered `&x` as the
# value itself would fail. This is the write half of the AddressOf/Deref pair contract.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): allocation and freshness (addresses are GIVEN, not
# minted — minting a fresh one is the allocator's job); aliasing freedom in the source
# language (a heap where two addresses could denote one cell needs an alias model this
# theory does not have); and lifetime/GC.
spec: TextOps#address_of_sound
